* Configuration
The main configuration file is:
  /etc/logrotate.conf

Additional per-service rules can be placed in:
  /etc/logrotate.d/

The default configuration includes this directory.

Example:

/var/log/example.log {
    weekly
    rotate 4
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
}


* Testing the configuration
Check the configuration without performing any rotation:
  logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf

Force a rotation regardless of the configured interval:
  logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.conf

The default state file is:
  /var/lib/logrotate.status


* Periodic execution
This port does not install or enable any automatic scheduling mechanism.

logrotate can be executed periodically using cron, for example once per day:
  0 0 * * * /usr/sbin/logrotate /etc/logrotate.conf

Adjust the schedule according to the needs of the system.

On CRUX, logrotate can be executed daily by creating an executable script at:
  /etc/cron/daily/logrotate

For example:

#!/bin/sh

/usr/sbin/logrotate /etc/logrotate.conf

Make the script executable:

  chmod 755 /etc/cron/daily/logrotate

Running logrotate daily is suitable even when individual rules use weekly,
monthly or other rotation intervals. logrotate uses its state file to decide
when each log actually needs to be rotated.



* Mail support
Upstream defaults to using /bin/mail for mail notifications.

A compatible mail command is required only when using the mail directive.
On CRUX, this functionality can be provided by installing a suitable mail
client such as mailx.

Mail support is optional and is not required for normal log rotation.
